## Route A: induction within valley delays

A useful genuine closure observation: decrementing all delays and deleting an eligible minimum preserves the valley order of surviving old leaves. A newly exposed leaf replaces its removed child at the same planar position and gets value1. Thus the recursive class stays within anti-unimodal delays.

However, the star proof in source Theorem2.3 depends on deletion giving an identical residual polynomial, up to known shifts. That fails for general trees. Take root0, parents[-1,0,0,2,0], leaves1,3,4 with delays(1,1,2). Deleting left leaf1 gives an undelayed two-branch tree with Q=[3]; deleting leaf3 exposes vertex2 and gives the undelayed three-star with Q=[3]!=1+2q+2q²+q³. Their r-values are3 and1 respectively. Consequently the literal answer is q³[3]+q[3]!=q+2q²+3q³+2q⁴+q⁵. There is no common residual factor from isomorphic deletions. This is a failed method, not a counterexample.

A shared-mode induction also cannot simply be assumed: in the undelayed tree with root branches of lengths1 and4 the two summands are q⁴ and [4], with disjoint sets of modes, although their sum [5] is unimodal.

Precise gap: a uniform sign-control or interlacing statement for these structurally related, differently shifted residual coefficient sequences. Closure under valley delays alone does not control their sum. Arbitrary nonnegative sums of unimodal polynomials need not be unimodal (1+q²).

## Route B: positive product / strong-unimodality mechanism

Unlike the star formula, the full-tree polynomial need not be a monomial times a product of symmetric quantum integers or Gaussian polynomials. Concrete literal tree: parents[-1,0,1,1,0,4,4], root with two children each having two leaves. Left-to-right leaf delays at vertices(2,3,5,6) are(3,1,1,2), a valley. Its exact polynomial is

    q+2q²+4q³+5q⁴+6q⁵+4q⁶+3q⁷+q⁸.

It is not symmetric after stripping its initial q, and coefficient6 violates log-concavity: 4²<6*3. Thus neither a universal symmetric-positive-product factorization nor a universal log-concavity proof can work. It remains unimodal. In particular this witness is NOT an answer to source4.2, and it is not a credited helper result.

Alternative exact formulation: legal plucking orders are linear extensions of the descendant-before-ancestor partial order, with the original leaf v forbidden before position f(v). Newly exposed leaves have no additional waiting condition. The exponent counts incomparable left-right vertex pairs for which the left vertex is removed first. This follows by charging each r(T,v) edge when v is removed; each incomparable pair is charged once or never, according to its planar/removal order. The fixed original-leaf release inequality is equivalent to repeated countdown because at step t its delay is max(1,f(v)-t+1).

This gives an ordinary finite combinatorial reformulation, not a solution: unimodality of this restricted linear-extension enumerator for every valley release vector remains unproved. Ordinary undelayed q-hook/unimodality results do not remove release restrictions. Independent direct enumeration of only the4- and6-edge witnesses (24 and720 permutations) verifies their literal recursive polynomials and guards against a misleading exponent/leaf-order interpretation.

## Diagnostics and strongest objection

12 deliberately chosen three-path release-time probes and6 fixed branching probes: all unimodal. These are structural diagnostics, not a universal search or proof. No order grid expansion. Both source witnesses above are exact integer computations; independent.py checks them by the distinct linear-extension method. Check.py also verifies the n2 local Gram determinant and49 fixed smoothing supports.

Strongest falsifier for any claimed completion: produce a valley-delay tree whose coefficient first differences turn from negative back to positive. None found here. Strongest present objection: the available source statements and known undelayed formulas have not supplied a uniform relation among differently shifted residual polynomials. That gap survives both induction and product/log-concavity routes. It does not establish impossibility of solving the target.

